{"kind":"artifact","text":"Every MCP tool declares the shape of the structuredContent it already returns, and a named test now fails if that declaration stops being honest. Exact clean pushed head e42464351daf00439b63d52873721985e8dae899 on request/mcp-output-schema-v2; tree ff58098e5acd3ffdc5451f5ec0cb98d474fe02d0; first parent 3f35d6edf4d0d81002ed1c3dab6d7e80ba27c0f5, which is current main, so this is a one-commit fast-forward. Under planner's request 926cbf9e and my promise ab930730, and the commit's Rests-On names 926cbf9e, not the superseded 206708ca.\n\nWHAT THIS IS FOR, STATED THE WAY IT SURVIVES SCRUTINY. This is conformance and self-description, not the repair of an observed breakage. The motivating report was that a client could not read structuredContent; hugh researched it and the version of kiro he used has no structuredContent support at all, with or without an advertised outputSchema. So this change would not have fixed the case that produced the row, and nothing here claims otherwise. What stands on its own: the MCP specification provides outputSchema precisely to declare the shape of structuredContent, gitseq returned structuredContent while declaring nothing about its shape, and a client that gates on the declaration now has one. That framing is in the source comment and the commit message, not only here — the previous head corrected it in the artifact and left the old claim in the tree, which was the worse half of that mistake because the tree is what a maintainer reads.\n\nTHE PERMISSIVENESS IS NOW PROVED RATHER THAN ASSERTED, and this is the defect codex found in the previous head. The schema is {\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":true} on all fourteen tools, attached by one post-definition loop with a fresh map per tool so a tool added later cannot silently miss it. I had called that permissiveness the deliberate heart of the design and then left it untested, so flipping additionalProperties from true to false kept every test green while making ordinary non-empty structuredContent invalid against the very schema being declared.\n\nTestEveryToolDeclaresAnOutputSchemaItsOwnResponsesSatisfy now closes that. It drives a real tools/list and five real tools/call requests over the existing httptest-backed harness, requires each response to be non-error with NON-EMPTY structuredContent, and validates each payload against its own tool's declared schema. The five cover both payload shapes deliberately: whoami and presence return a Go map, while status, work and artifacts return statusview value structs, including a paged tool whose page comes back empty.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD RATHER THAN RELAYED. I flipped additionalProperties to false at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go line 638 and ran the named test: exit 1, \"tool whoami returns structuredContent its own outputSchema rejects: member degraded is not named by the schema, which admits no others\". I restored the line, confirmed the working tree clean against the committed tree, and re-ran: exit 0. Which unnamed member the failure names varies with map iteration order — an earlier run of the same mutation named \"protocol\" instead of \"degraded\" — so a reviewer reproducing this should expect the member to differ and the refusal not to. Dropping one tool from the attach loop also fails the same named test, reporting the tool that declares no outputSchema.\n\nNO KEY IS NAMED, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. A wrong schema is strictly worse than none: none degrades to today's behaviour, while a conformant client validating against a wrong one rejects a valid response. No key survives every return path — each resident-backed tool has a degraded local-fold fallback of a different shape, and submit conditionally adds degraded, warning and projected — so the declaration stops at what is proved.\n\nWHY type object IS ARGUED, NOT ASSUMED, AND WHY THE OLD REASON WAS FALSE. The previous head's test claimed the negotiated protocol admits only an object output schema. That is false: MCP 2026-07-28 defines outputSchema as JSON Schema generally, so the protocol forces nothing. Object is correct here for a narrower reason — every success path in dispatch returns either a Go map or a statusview value struct, both of which encode as JSON objects, and an object schema is also the shape older protocol revisions understand. The failure path carries no structuredContent at all, so there is nothing there to validate. That corrected reasoning is stated in the test's doc comment and in the commit message.\n\nNO SCHEMA LIBRARY WAS ADDED. go.mod has no JSON Schema dependency and I did not introduce one. The test carries a small in-file checker covering exactly the schema the adapter declares — type, the named properties, and whether additionalProperties admits anything else. Reviewer: this is the piece most worth attacking. If the checker is too lenient it would pass a schema that a real conformant client would reject, and the mutation evidence above would be worth less than it looks.\n\nGATES. The full suite was run by the implementing agent at this tree immediately before commit, with git status --porcelain empty across the commit so the gated tree is byte-identical to ff58098e: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero FAIL, including cmd/gitseq-mcp and internal/docset; gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0. I did not personally re-run the full suite after the commit — I ran the focused named test and both mutations myself at this exact head. Please re-run the suite rather than take the transcription.","body":{"commit":"e42464351daf00439b63d52873721985e8dae899","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}